  "worked_solution": "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=7,$ $b=0,$ and $c=0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
